de spending argue for keeping uncertainty wide rather than extending the margin.", "watch": "A late shift in the national midterm environment or Rogers consolidating swing-voter support could tighten this into a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat is one of the closest contests of the cycle, with Gary Peters retiring and Democrats nominating Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers. Polls from mid-to-late September, including Marist, NYT/Siena, and Suffolk, show El-Sayed leading by 3 to 7 points, with even Republican-sponsored surveys mostly finding a Democratic edge. Expert raters split between Tossup and Lean D, and prediction markets give El-Sayed roughly a 72-74% chance.\n\nThe race remains competitive because Rogers benefits from substantial outside support and Michigan's even partisan balance, while El-Sayed faces heavy opposition spending. The forecast expects a narrow Democratic win of about three to four points, with meaningful uncertainty given the open-seat dynamics and the possibility of a late environment shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7553, "margin": 3.4}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6955, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7118, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7565, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7778, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.755, "margin": 3.5}]}